{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"start_index":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests:\nndent International Fact-Finding Mission on the Islamic Republic of Iran\") released a report which found the Iranian regime committed systematic [crimes against humanity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity \"Crimes against humanity\").[[32]](#cite_note-35)[[33]](#cite_note-36)\n\n## Background\n\n### Condition of women and the Islamic Republic\n\nAmong the [primary tenets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Government \"Islamic Government\") of the 1979 [Iranian Islamic Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\") was the need to overthrow the (allegedly) anti-Islamic, oppressive and foreign power-controlled monarchy of Iran. The secular, modernizing [Pahlavi dynasty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pahlavi_dynasty \"Pahlavi dynasty\"), founded by [Reza Shah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Shah \"Reza Shah\") in the early twentieth century (1925), had placed the improved treatment of women at the center of its project to modernize Iran. Reza banned the wearing of [hijab](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hijab \"Hijab\") in public and admitted women to universities. During the reign of his successor and son [Mohammad Reza](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Reza_Pahlavi \"Mohammad Reza Pahlavi\"), restrictions on the wearing of hijab were lifted but women were granted [suffrage](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suffrage \"Suffrage\"), allowed to enter parliament, and \"gained dramatically more rights in marriage\".[[note 4]](#cite_note-37)\n\nThe [Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\") rescinded the legal rights the Pahlavi Shahs granted women, removed restrictions on men's rights to polygamy and child marriage, and reversed Reza's ban on hijab to make [the complete covering of women's hair in public](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hijab_in_Iran \"Hijab in Iran\") compulsory. Enforcement of the unpopular law was eased during the 2013–2021 tenure of moderate President [Rouhani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassan_Rouhani \"Hassan Rouhani\"), but intensified under Rouhani's successor, the hard-line President [Ebrahim Raisi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebrahim_Raisi \"Ebrahim Raisi\").[[34]](#cite_note-38)\n\nAt the same time as rights were taken away, some aspects of the lives of women and girls improved. Traditional pious Iranians—who had kept their daughters out of school during the Shah's era—now felt comfortable allowing their daughters to be schooled in an Islamic educational system. Enrollment of women in universities jumped from 3% in 1977 to 67% in 2015, according to the [World Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Bank \"World Bank\") statistics. But many of the women who left home to study and developed new values and world views, struggled to secure jobs that matched their new competencies,[[17]](#cite_note-Moaveni-NYer-7-8-2023-17) and became less content with the Islamic Republic than their parents.\n\n### Protest movements under the Islamic Republic\n\nThe Mahsa Amini protests were preceded by several other political/social/economic protest movements in Iran, in [1999](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_student_protests,_July_1999 \"Iran student protests, July 1999\"), [2009](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Iranian_presidential_election_protests \"2009 Iranian presidential election protests\"), [2011–2012](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011%E2%80%932012_Iranian_protests \"2011–2012 Iranian protests\"), [2019–2020](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019%E2%80%932020_Iranian_protests \"2019–2020 Iranian protests\"), and [protests against compulsory hijab](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_protests_against_compulsory_hijab \"Iranian protests against compulsory hijab\") in 2017–18.\nMost recently were a series of protests in 2019, \"[Bloody Aban](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019%E2%80%932020_Iranian_protests \"2019–2020 Iranian protests\")\" ([Persian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language \"Persian language\"): آبان خونین, a month near November in the [Iranian calendar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_calendar \"Iranian calendar\")), sparked by a 50–200% increase in fuel prices.[[35]](#cite_note-39) Like the Mahsa Amini protests, these protests eventually called for the overthrow of the [government of the Islamic Republic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran \"Government of the Islamic Republic of Iran\") and [Supreme Leader](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran \"Supreme Leader of Iran\") [Ali Khamenei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\").[[36]](#cite_note-auto12-40)[[37]](#cite_note-:03-41)\n\n### Mahsa Amini's arrest and death in custody\n\nMahsa Amini, also Jina Amini, a 22-year-old [Kurdish Iranian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_Iranian \"Kurdish Iranian\") woman, was arrested by the [Guidance Patrol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guidance_Patrol \"Guidance Patrol\") on 14 September 2022 because of an \"improper hijab.\" The police were accused of beating her and inflicting a fatal head injury,[[38]](#cite_note-guardian220919-42) after a [CT scan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CT_scan \"CT scan\") confirmed that Amini had sustained head injuries, a claim denied by Tehran police.[[38]](#cite_note-guardian220919-42) She was pronounced dead on 16 September.\n\n## Protests\n\nProtests started in Tehran as Mahsa Amini was being treated at a hospital there, and continued at her funeral in Saqqez, where hundreds of people reportedly gathered in defiance of official warnings and were fired upon when they shouted anti-regime slogans.[[39]](#cite_note-guardian_17_sep-43)[[40]](#cite_note-:11-44) Protests spread to the provincial capital. Internet service and mobile phone service were shut down, and the head of the [Guidance Patrol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guidance_Patrol \"Guidance Patrol\") was allegedly suspended.[[38]](#cite_note-guardian220919-42) By 20 September there were unconfirmed social media videos showed anti-government protests in at least 16 of Iran's 31 provinces, and state media reported that three people had been killed in Kurdistan protests.[[41]](#cite_note-45)\n\nTwo weeks after the funeral, forty civilian were killed and many wounded in [Zahedan in Sistan and Baluchestan province following Friday prayers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Zahedan_attack \"2022 Zahedan attack\"),[[42]](#cite_note-Wintour2-46) after protests sparked by reports of a police chief who had raped a 15-year-old girl in [Chahbahar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chahbahar \"Chahbahar\").[[43]](#cite_note-Rezaei-47)[[42]](#cite_note-Wintour2-46) A Mahsa Amini hashtag gained 52 million tweets.[[44]](#cite_note-Narratives-CNN-5-10-22-48)\nBy early December 2022, a \"vague\" statement made by the attorney general was interpreted by some in the Western press to mean that the hijab law was under review and that the [Guidance Patrol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guidance_Patrol \"Guidance Patrol\") might be disbanded.[[45]](#cite_note-disband-49) The report was later attacked as a \"diversion tactic\" by the regime.[[46]](#cite_note-CBC-Tizhoosh-5-12-2022-50)\n\nThe protests became more widespread than those of 2009, 2017, and 2019, encompassing even Islamic Republic power bases such as the holy cities of [Mashhad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad \"Mashhad\") and [Qom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qom \"Qom\"),[[47]](#cite_note-51) involving both urban middle classes and rural working areas. In addition, schoolgirls demonstrated in numbers for the first time.[[48]](#cite_note-bbc_guide-52)\n\nBy February 2023, the regime stated it had arrested tens of thousands of protesters.[[49]](#cite_note-AP-pardoned-13-3-2023-53)\n\nAccording to [France 24](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France_24 \"France 24\"), by mid-March 2023 protests \"had dwindled\" across most of the country.[[12]](#cite_note-Baloch-F24-20-3-23-12) On 13 March, the government claimed it had pardoned 22,000 citizens arrested for protesting.[[49]](#cite_note-AP-pardoned-13-3-2023-53)\n\n### Slogans and grievances\n\nThe signature slogan of the protests has been \"[Woman, Life, Freedom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woman,_Life,_Freedom \"Woman, Life, Freedom\")\" which reflects the idea that \"the rights of women are at the centre of life and liberty\".[[50]](#cite_note-Britannica-54)\nThe slogan was popularized during women's marches in Turkey in 2006 in the Kurdish Freedom Movement, and reflects the idea of Kurdish leader [Abdullah Ocalan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdullah_Ocalan \"Abdullah Ocalan\"), that \"a country can't be free unless the women are free.\"\n[[51]](#cite_note-Bayram-NPR-27-10-22-55)\n\n[Kurds](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurds \"Kurds\") emphasize the Kurdish origin of the slogan, and its connection to Mahsa Amini's Kurdish first name: Jina.[[51]](#cite_note-Bayram-NPR-27-10-22-55) In [Kurdish](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_languages \"Kurdish languages\"), the word *jin* means \"woman\".[[52]](#cite_note-56) Kurdish and minority rights are subjects of the protest—Kurds make up approximately 15% of Iran's population, are predominately Sunni Muslims (also a minority), the regions they live are among the most impoverished in the country, the use of [their languages](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_languages \"Kurdish languages\") is restricted, they account for nearly half of all political prisoners in Iran, and have felt much of the brunt of anti-protest attacks by the government (which attacked predominantly Kurdish cities, like Sanandaj and Oshnavieh) who according to news reports, blamed the Kurds for the protest movement.[[51]](#cite_note-Bayram-NPR-27-10-22-55)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Antigovernment_protests_after_the_death_of_Mahsa_Amini_in_Hamedan_-_September_2022_%2802%29.jpg/250px-Antigovernment_protests_after_the_death_of_Mahsa_Amini_in_Hamedan_-_September_2022_%2802%29.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Antigovernment_protests_after_the_death_of_Mahsa_Amini_in_Hamedan_-_Se\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 20000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
