{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"start_index":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_program_of_Iran"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_program_of_Iran:\ncritical, initially fueled with 93% highly enriched uranium (HEU) provided by the US. Iran became one of the original signatories of the NPT when it entered into force in March 1970, committing as a non-nuclear-weapon state not to pursue nuclear arms.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nBy the mid-1970s, the Shah expanded the program, citing the need for \"alternative sources of fuel for the day when the oil reserves would be depleted.\"[[23]](#cite_note-24) In 1974 he established the [Atomic Energy Organization of Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_Energy_Organization_of_Iran \"Atomic Energy Organization of Iran\") (AEOI) and announced plans to produce 23,000 megawatts of electricity from a network of nuclear power plants over 20 years. Contracts were signed with Western firms: Iran paid over $1 billion for a 10% stake in the French [Eurodif](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurodif \"Eurodif\") consortium's uranium enrichment plant, and [West Germany](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Germany \"West Germany\")'s [Kraftwerk Union](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siemens \"Siemens\") agreed to build two 1,200 MWe pressurized water reactors at [Bushehr](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bushehr \"Bushehr\"). Construction of the [Bushehr Nuclear Power Plant](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bushehr_Nuclear_Power_Plant \"Bushehr Nuclear Power Plant\") began in 1975, and Iran also negotiated with France's Framatome to supply additional reactors. Plans were made for a full domestic nuclear fuel cycle, including uranium mining and fuel fabrication, with a new Nuclear Technology Center established at Isfahan.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\n### Post-revolution revival and war impact (1979–1980s)\n\nThis ambitious program slowed dramatically after the [1979 Islamic Revolution](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\"). The Shah was deposed and Iran's new leaders under Ayatollah [Ruhollah Khomeini](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruhollah_Khomeini \"Ruhollah Khomeini\") were initially hostile to nuclear technology, seeing it as a symbol of Western influence. Many ongoing nuclear projects were shelved or canceled. The [Iran–Iraq War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran–Iraq_War \"Iran–Iraq War\") (1980–1988) derailed the nuclear program: resources were diverted to the war effort, and Iraq targeted Iran's nuclear infrastructure. The partially built Bushehr reactor site was bombed multiple times by Iraqi warplanes, and Siemens withdrew from the project, leaving the reactor shells heavily damaged. By the late 1980s, Iran's nuclear program had effectively been put on hold.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\n### Secret expansion and weaponization efforts (1990s–2002)\n\nBy the early 1990s, Iran's nuclear program accelerated on two parallel tracks: one overtly civilian and one covert. Openly, Iran continued working with Russia and China to build peaceful nuclear infrastructure. Bushehr's reactor project moved forward under Russian engineers (though plagued by delays until it finally came online in 2011), and China helped Iran with nuclear research and uranium mining expertise. Less transparently, Iran was building a secret enrichment capability and exploring technologies relevant to nuclear weapons, away from the eyes of inspectors.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nIran's covert procurement of enrichment technology bore fruit in the 1990s. Thousands of centrifuge components, tools, and technical drawings obtained from [A.Q. Khan](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdul_Qadeer_Khan \"Abdul Qadeer Khan\") [proliferation network](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_proliferation \"Nuclear proliferation\") were used to set up secret pilot enrichment workshops. Experiments with [uranium hexafluoride gas](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uranium_hexafluoride \"Uranium hexafluoride\") were conducted in undeclared facilities in Tehran (such as the Kalaye Electric Company) in the late 1990s. In 2000, Iran completed a uranium conversion plant at Isfahan, based on a Chinese design, to produce uranium hexafluoride feedstock for enrichment. It also developed domestic sources of uranium: the [Saghand](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saghand \"Saghand\") mine in [Yazd province](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yazd_province \"Yazd province\") (with Chinese assistance) and the [Gchine](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gchine_uranium_mine \"Gchine uranium mine\") mine and mill near the Gulf coast. The Gchine uranium mine became operational in 2004 and is now believed to have originally been part of a military-run nuclear effort, kept hidden from the IAEA until revealed in 2003. These steps gave Iran independent access to the raw materials and precursor processes for a weapons-capable nuclear fuel cycle.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nIn the late 1990s Iran launched a nuclear weapons research program, codenamed the [AMAD Project](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AMAD_Project \"AMAD Project\"), under the aegis of the Iranian Ministry of Defense. According to later IAEA findings, the AMAD Project (led by [Mohsen Fakhrizadeh](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohsen_Fakhrizadeh \"Mohsen Fakhrizadeh\"), a top nuclear scientist) aimed to design and build an arsenal of five nuclear warheads by the mid-2000s. Between 1999 and 2003, this secret program managed to acquire warhead design (reportedly and possibly including an engineered [Pakistan design](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistan_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction \"Pakistan and weapons of mass destruction\") from KRL– although Pakistan disputed this claim), conducting high-explosive tests and detonator development for an implosion-type bomb, manufacturing some nuclear weapon components with surrogate materials, and integrating a warhead design into Iran's [Shahab-3](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahab-3 \"Shahab-3\") ballistic missile system. The main thing Amad lacked was [fissile material](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fissile_material \"Fissile material\"), since Iran had not yet produced weapons-grade uranium or plutonium for a bomb core. Still, the scope of Amad demonstrated that Iran was exploring the bomb option in violation of its NPT obligations.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nThroughout the 1990s, Iranian entities also received steady assistance from foreign sources. Some Russian and Chinese companies provided Iran with expertise and equipment for its nuclear projects. For example, Chinese technicians conducted uranium exploration in Iran and allegedly supplied blueprints that aided Iran's construction of the Isfahan conversion facility. Iran's scientists also gained know-how from Pakistan's secret network and from academic exchanges abroad. That enabled Iran to secretly establish the critical facilities that could produce weapons-usable material: large uranium enrichment plants and a heavy-water reactor project.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nBy the early 2000s, two key clandestine facilities were nearing completion: a uranium enrichment center at [Natanz](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natanz \"Natanz\") (in central Iran), built to house thousands of centrifuges, and a [heavy water](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heavy_water \"Heavy water\") production plant alongside a 40 MW heavy-water reactor ([IR-40](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IR-40 \"IR-40\")) near [Arak](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arak,_Iran \"Arak, Iran\"). These facilities, which had been kept secret from the IAEA, were intended for ostensibly civilian purposes but had clear weapons potential. Enrichment at Natanz could yield high-enriched uranium for bombs, while the Arak reactor (once operational) could produce plutonium in its spent fuel, and the heavy water plant would supply the reactor's coolant. In August 2002, an exiled [Iranian opposition](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_opposition \"Iranian opposition\") group, the [National Council of Resistance of Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Council_of_Resistance_of_Iran \"National Council of Resistance of Iran\") (NCRI), exposed the existence of Natanz and Arak. Satellite imagery soon confirmed construction at these sites. The revelation that Iran had built major nuclear facilities in secret, without required disclosure to the IAEA, ignited an international crisis and raised questions about the program's true aim.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\n### Exposure and International Confrontation (2002–2013)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Tehran_Declaration_-_21_October_2003_-_Joschka_Fischer%2C_Hassan_Rouhani%2C_Dominique_de_Villepin_and_Jack_Straw.png/250px-Tehran_Declaration_-_21_October_2003_-_Joschka_Fischer%2C_Hassan_Rouhani%2C_Dominique_de_Villepin_and_Jack_Straw.png)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tehran_Declaration_-_21_October_2003_-_Joschka_Fischer,_Hassan_Rouhani,_Dominique_de_Villepin_and_Jack_Straw.png)\n\nThe Tehran Declaration on 21 October 2003, from right to left: [Joschka Fischer](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joschka_Fischer \"Joschka Fischer\"), [Hassan Rouhani](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassan_Rouhani \"Hassan Rouhani\"), [Dominique de Villepin](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominique_de_Villepin \"Dominique de Villepin\") and [Jack Straw](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Straw \"Jack Straw\").\n\nIn late 2003, Iran was facing the prospect of censure and agreed to a degree of cooperation. In October 2003, Iran and the foreign ministers of Britain, France, and Germany (the \"EU-3\") struck the Tehran Agreement: Iran pledged to temporarily suspend all uranium enrichment and reprocessing activities, allow more intrusive inspections by signing the Additional Protocol, and clarify past nuclear work. This deal, reached just ahead of an IAEA Board of Governors deadline, was intended to build confidence while a longer-term solution was negotiated. However, Iran's cooperation was halting and incomplete.[[22]](#cite_note-iranwatch20231219-23)\n\nIn 2004 and 2005, the IAEA uncovered inconsistencies and omissions in Iran's disclosures, such as experiments with plutonium separation and advanced P-2 centrifuge designs that Iran had failed to report. Iran's suspension of enrichment proved short-lived as \n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 20000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatwa_against_nuclear_weapons"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatwa_against_nuclear_weapons"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatwa_against_nuclear_weapons:\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nA [fatwa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatwa \"Fatwa\") by [Ali Khamenei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\"), then [supreme leader of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_leader_of_Iran \"Supreme leader of Iran\"), against the development of [nuclear weapons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_weapons \"Nuclear weapons\")[[1]](#cite_note-AT-1) dates back to the mid-1990s.[[2]](#cite_note-forein_policy-2) The first public announcement is reported to have occurred in October 2003, followed by an official statement at a meeting of the [International Atomic Energy Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Atomic_Energy_Agency \"International Atomic Energy Agency\") (IAEA) in [Vienna](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna \"Vienna\"), Austria, in August 2005.\n\nSome analysts have questioned either the existence, applicability and/or constancy of the fatwa.[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3)[[4]](#cite_note-Khalaji-4) According to [Mehdi Khalaji](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehdi_Khalaji \"Mehdi Khalaji\"), Khamenei may alter his fatwa under critical circumstances, as did his predecessor, [Ruhollah Khomeini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruhollah_Khomeini \"Ruhollah Khomeini\"), on some civil and political issues.[[4]](#cite_note-Khalaji-4) According to [Gareth Porter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gareth_Porter \"Gareth Porter\") writing in *[Foreign Policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_Policy \"Foreign Policy\")*, Iran's aversion to nuclear and chemical weapons is sincere because of the \"historical episode during its eight-year [war with Iraq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Iraq_War \"Iran–Iraq War\")\", and Iran never sought revenge for [Iraqi chemical attacks against Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iraqi_chemical_attacks_against_Iran \"Iraqi chemical attacks against Iran\"), which killed 20,000 Iranians and severely injured 100,000 more.[[2]](#cite_note-forein_policy-2) According to Khalaji, the fatwa is also considered to be consistent with Islamic tradition.[[4]](#cite_note-Khalaji-4)\n\nAs an Istifta'at[[a]](#cite_note-6) the policy is included on Khamenei's official website,[[6]](#cite_note-khamenei.ir-7) and was referred to in remarks by both U.S. president [Barack Obama](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama \"Barack Obama\")[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3)[[7]](#cite_note-USA_today-8)[[8]](#cite_note-WH-9) and Khamenei himself.[[9]](#cite_note-10) In 2021, acknowledging the fatwa, Iranian intelligence minister [Esmaeil Khatib](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esmaeil_Khatib \"Esmaeil Khatib\") said that the country may nevertheless change their stance if \"pushed in that direction\" like a \"cornered cat\".[[10]](#cite_note-11)[[11]](#cite_note-12) During a February 2025 meeting between Khamenei and the IRGC, commanders pressured Khamenei to rescind his ban on nuclear weapons.[[12]](#cite_note-13) In March 2025, Khamenei's advisor [Ali Larijani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Larijani \"Ali Larijani\") said Iran would have no choice but to develop nuclear weapons if attacked by the [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") or its allies.[[13]](#cite_note-14)\n\nAccording to Gareth Porter, the fatwa was issued for the first time in the mid-1990s in a letter that was never publicly released. The fatwa was issued \"without any fanfare\" responding to a request from an official \"for his religious opinion on nuclear weapons\".[[2]](#cite_note-forein_policy-2)\n\nIn October 2003, Khamenei issued an oral fatwa that forbade the production and use of any form of [weapon of mass destruction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weapon_of_mass_destruction \"Weapon of mass destruction\").[[4]](#cite_note-Khalaji-4)\nTwo years later, in August 2005, the fatwa was cited in an official statement by the Iranian government at a meeting of the [International Atomic Energy Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Atomic_Energy_Agency \"International Atomic Energy Agency\") (IAEA) in [Vienna](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna \"Vienna\"). It stated that the production, stockpiling and use of [nuclear weapons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_weapon \"Nuclear weapon\") were forbidden under Islam.[[14]](#cite_note-IAEA-15)\n\n[Iran's nuclear program](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran's_nuclear_program \"Iran's nuclear program\") has been a subject of international debate for decades. The Iranian government claims that the purpose of its nuclear development is to produce electricity, and Khamenei said that it fundamentally rejects nuclear weapons, but experts believe that Iran is technically able to enrich uranium for producing a bomb within a few months.[[15]](#cite_note-16)\n\nFour days after the [Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Comprehensive_Plan_of_Action \"Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action\") (JCPOA) agreement, Khamenei delivered a speech, highlighting his fatwa and rejecting the claim that the nuclear talks, rather than Iran's religious abstinence, prevented Iran from acquiring nuclear weapons:\n\n> The Americans say they stopped Iran from acquiring a nuclear weapon. They know it's not true. We had a fatwa (religious ruling), declaring nuclear weapons to be religiously forbidden under Islamic law. It had nothing to do with the nuclear talks.[[16]](#cite_note-Telegraph-17)\n\n## Official statements\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fatwa_against_nuclear_weapons&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Official statements\")]\n\nThe Iranian official website for information on its nuclear program has provided numerous instances of public statements by Khamenei in which he voices his opposition to pursuit and development of nuclear weapons in moral, religious and Islamic juridical terms.[[17]](#cite_note-18) Khamenei's official website specifically cites a 2010 version[[18]](#cite_note-19) of those statements in the fatwa section of the website in [Farsi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farsi \"Farsi\") as a fatwa on \"Prohibition of Weapons of Mass Destruction\":[[6]](#cite_note-khamenei.ir-7)\n\n> We believe that besides nuclear weapons, other types of weapons of mass destruction such as chemical and biological weapons also pose a serious threat to humanity. The Iranian nation which is itself a victim of chemical weapons feels more than any other nation the danger that is caused by the production and stockpiling of such weapons and is prepared to make use of all its facilities to counter such threats.\n> We consider the use of such weapons as haraam and believe that it is everyone's duty to make efforts to secure humanity against this great disaster.[[6]](#cite_note-khamenei.ir-7)\n\nAlso, he said during a speech delivered on 9 April 2015 in a meeting with a group of panegyrists:\n\n> This is while (sic) we are not after nuclear tests. We are not after nuclear weapons. And this is not because they are telling us not to pursue these things. Rather, we do not want these things for the sake of ourselves and our religion and because reason is telling us not to do so. Both shar'i and aqli [related to logic and reason] fatwas dictate that we do not pursue them. Our aqli fatwa is that we do not need a nuclear weapon either in the present time or in the future. A nuclear weapon is a source of trouble for a country like ours.[[19]](#cite_note-20)\n\nThe fatwa has been widely discussed by international officials and was referred to in remarks by U.S. president [Barack Obama](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama \"Barack Obama\").[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3)[[7]](#cite_note-USA_today-8)[[8]](#cite_note-WH-9)\n\nIn a statement on a conversation with [Hasan Rouhani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasan_Rouhani \"Hasan Rouhani\"), Obama said:\n\n> Iran's supreme leader has issued a fatwa against the development of nuclear weapons.[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3)\n\nA similar statement is quoted from [John Kerry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Kerry \"John Kerry\"), saying:\n\n> So I close by saying to all of you that the singular objective that brought us to Geneva remains our singular objective as we leave Geneva, and that is to ensure that Iran does not acquire a nuclear weapon. In that singular object, we are resolute. Foreign Minister [Mohammad Javad] Zarif emphasized that they don't intend to do this, and the supreme leader has indicated there is a fatwa, which forbids them to do this.[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3)\n\nThe fatwa is regarded as consistent with a set of rules in Islamic tradition that prohibit weapons that indiscriminately kill women, children and the elderly.[[4]](#cite_note-Khalaji-4)\n\nQuestions have been raised by some [think tanks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Think_tank \"Think tank\") affiliated with the US and Israel on the fatwa's existence, authenticity and impact[[7]](#cite_note-USA_today-8) and on whether it was only a political statement that lacks the authority of a religiously binding fatwa.[[3]](#cite_note-Kessler-7-4-15-3) [James Risen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Risen \"James Risen\") of *[The New York Times](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times \"The New York Times\")* noted that Khamenei said \"that it was a mistake for Col. Muammar el-[Qaddafi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qaddafi \"Qaddafi\") of [Libya](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libya \"Libya\") to give up his nuclear weapons program\". Some analysts raised the possibility that Khamenei might be lying by using [taqiyya](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taqiyya \"Taqiyya\"), which is a form of religious dissembling.[[20]](#cite_note-21)[[21]](#cite_note-22) In 2015, an open letter to Obama, posted on *[Iranian.com](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian.com \"Iranian.com\")*, reportedly from a nephew of Ali Khamenei, stated that Khamenei practiced taqiyya with regard to the fatwa.[[22]](#cite_note-23) [Gholam-Hossein Elham](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholam-Hossein_Elham \"Gholam-Hossein Elham\"), an Iranian politician, argu\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Israel_conflict_during_the_Syrian_civil_war"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Israel_conflict_during_the_Syrian_civil_war"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Israel_conflict_during_the_Syrian_civil_war:\n## Timeline\n\n### 2013\n\nOn 30 January 2013, Israeli aircraft allegedly [struck a Syrian convoy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_2013_Rif_Dimashq_airstrike \"January 2013 Rif Dimashq airstrike\") transporting Iranian weapons to Hezbollah.[[8]](#cite_note-JPRifattack-11) Other sources stated the targeted site was a [military research center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_Scientific_Studies_and_Research_Center \"Syrian Scientific Studies and Research Center\") in [Jamraya](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamraya \"Jamraya\") responsible for developing biological and chemical weapons.[[14]](#cite_note-17)\n\nTwo additional [air strikes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/May_2013_Rif_Dimashq_airstrikes \"May 2013 Rif Dimashq airstrikes\"), also attributed to Israel, reportedly took place on 3 and 5 May 2013. Both allegedly targeted long-ranged weapons sent from Iran to Hezbollah.[[15]](#cite_note-18)[[16]](#cite_note-19)\n\nAccording to anonymous US officials, Israel launched another [attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/July_2013_Latakia_explosion \"July 2013 Latakia explosion\") on 5 July. It allegedly targeted Russian-made [Yakhont](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P-800_Oniks \"P-800 Oniks\") anti-ship missiles near the city of [Latakia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latakia \"Latakia\") and killed several Syrian troops. While initial reports claimed that it was an airstrike, Israeli media later reported that the attack had been carried out with cruise missiles launched from a [Dolphin class submarine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolphin_class_submarine \"Dolphin class submarine\").[[17]](#cite_note-20)[[18]](#cite_note-21)\n\nAn unidentified U.S. administration official on 31 October said Israeli warplanes struck a Syrian base near the port of Latakia, targeting missiles that Israel thought might be transferred to its Lebanese militia enemy Hezbollah.[[19]](#cite_note-22)\n\nThe relationship between the [Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popular_Front_for_the_Liberation_of_Palestine \"Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine\") (PFLP) and the Islamic Republic of Iran strengthened as a result of Hamas moving away from Iran due to differing positions in the Syrian Civil War. Iran rewarded the Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine's [pro-Assad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bashar_al-Assad \"Bashar al-Assad\") stance with an increase in financial and military assistance. Abu Ahmad Fouad, a PFLP political bureau member said that the group might retaliate toward Israel if the United States bombs Syria.[[20]](#cite_note-23)\n\nOn 15 December 2013, a Lebanese sniper [opened fire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanikra_border_clash \"Hanikra border clash\") at an Israeli vehicle traveling near the border area of [Rosh Hanikra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosh_HaNikra_Crossing \"Rosh HaNikra Crossing\"), killing a soldier inside. Several hours later, the Israeli military said it shot two Lebanese soldiers after spotting \"suspicious movement\" in the same area.[[21]](#cite_note-24)\n\n### 2014\n\n[Syrian opposition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_opposition \"Syrian opposition\") sources, as well as Lebanese sources, reported that another strike happened in Latakia on 26 January 2014. Explosions were reported in the city and Israeli planes were reported over Lebanon. The target was allegedly [S-300](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S-300_missile_system \"S-300 missile system\") missiles.[[22]](#cite_note-25)\n\nIt was reported that Israeli aircraft carried out two airstrikes against Hezbollah facilities in Lebanon near the border with Syria on 24 February 2014, killing several militants. The [Syrian Observatory for Human Rights](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_Observatory_for_Human_Rights \"Syrian Observatory for Human Rights\") claimed the attack targeted a Hezbollah missile base.[[23]](#cite_note-26)\n\nOn 7 December 2014, Israeli jets allegedly bombed areas near [Damascus international airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damascus_International_Airport \"Damascus International Airport\") and in the town of [Dimas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Dimas \"Al-Dimas\"), near the border with Lebanon. According to foreign reports, the attack targeted a warehouse of advanced S-300 missiles, which were en route from Syria to Hezbollah in Lebanon.[[24]](#cite_note-27) [Al Arabiya](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al_Arabiya \"Al Arabiya\") reported that two Hezbollah militants were killed in the strikes, including a senior military official.[[25]](#cite_note-28)\n\n### 2015\n\nOn 18 January 2015, Israeli helicopters [allegedly attacked a Hezbollah's convoy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_2015_Mazraat_Amal_incident \"January 2015 Mazraat Amal incident\") in the Syrian-controlled part of Golan Heights, killing six prominent members of Hezbollah and six [IRGC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRGC \"IRGC\") commanders, including a General.[[26]](#cite_note-29)[[27]](#cite_note-30) On 28 January, Hezbollah [fired an anti-tank missile](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_2015_Shebaa_farms_incident \"January 2015 Shebaa farms incident\") at an Israeli military convoy in the [Shebaa farms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shebaa_Farms \"Shebaa Farms\"), killing two soldiers and wounding seven.[[28]](#cite_note-31) Israel responded with at least 50 artillery shells across the border into southern Lebanon, in which a Spanish [UN peacekeeper](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_peacekeeping \"United Nations peacekeeping\") was killed.[[29]](#cite_note-32)\n\nOn 25 April 2015, a [series of attacks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/April_2015_Qalamoun_incident \"April 2015 Qalamoun incident\") attributed to the Israeli Air Force was made in the [al-Qalamoun](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Qalamoun \"Al-Qalamoun\") region of Syria against Hezbollah camps and weapons convoys in two brigade bases.[[30]](#cite_note-33) [Al-Nusra Front](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Nusra_Front \"Al-Nusra Front\"), however, has also claimed the attacks.[[31]](#cite_note-34)\n\nOn 29 July 2015, Israeli airplanes reportedly struck a vehicle located in a [Druze](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze \"Druze\") village in southwestern Syria, killing Hezbollah men and a pro-Assad militiaman.[[32]](#cite_note-35) A second airstrike targeted a military base along the Syrian-Lebanese border belonging to a pro-Syrian Palestinian faction.[[33]](#cite_note-36)\n\nOn 20 and 21 August 2015, after four rockets hit the Golan Heights and [Upper Galilee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_Galilee \"Upper Galilee\"), Israel allegedly launched airstrikes in Syria, killing several militants.[[34]](#cite_note-37)\n\nAccording to Syrian media, on 31 October 2015, Israeli aircraft attacked numerous Hezbollah targets in southern Syria, close to the border with Lebanon in the [Qalamoun Mountains](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qalamoun_Mountains \"Qalamoun Mountains\") region. Estimated targets included a weapons convoy destined for Hezbollah.[[35]](#cite_note-38) It was reported another Israeli airstrike near Damascus airport on 11 November[[36]](#cite_note-39) that targeted Hezbollah weapons warehouses.[[37]](#cite_note-40)\n\nThe Syrian opposition reported an Israeli airstrike in the Qualamoun area of the Syria–Lebanon border on 23 November 2015. According to these sources, the strike killed 13 Syrian troops and Hezbollah fighters, and left dozens wounded, including four seriously. The Qualamoun region has been a major transit point for Hezbollah fighters and other logistical equipment to and from Syria.[[38]](#cite_note-41) According to Syrian sources, Israeli aircraft attacked again the [Syrian army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_Army \"Syrian Army\") and Hezbollah targets in the area around Qalamoun on 28 November, causing dead and wounded among Hezbollah fighters.[[39]](#cite_note-42)\n\nOn 19 December 2015, eight people, including [Samir Kuntar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samir_Kuntar \"Samir Kuntar\") and other Hezbollah commanders, were killed by an explosion on the outskirts of Damascus. According to Syrian government sources, Kuntar was killed by a \"terrorist rocket attack\".[[40]](#cite_note-sana-43)[*[unreliable source?](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Reliable_sources \"Wikipedia:Reliable sources\")*] Hezbollah claimed that the building was destroyed by an [air-to-surface missile](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air-to-surface_missile \"Air-to-surface missile\") launched by [Israeli Air Force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israeli_Air_Force \"Israeli Air Force\") [jets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warplane \"Warplane\").[[41]](#cite_note-44)[[42]](#cite_note-45)[[43]](#cite_note-auto-46) On 21 December, the [Free Syrian Army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_Syrian_Army \"Free Syrian Army\") released a video clip claiming responsibility for killing Kuntar.[[44]](#cite_note-47)[[45]](#cite_note-fsa-48)\n\nSources affiliated with the Syrian opposition reported that Israeli aircraft attacked seven positions belonging to Hezbollah in the Qalamoun Mountains area on 26 December 2015.[[46]](#cite_note-49)\n\n### 2016\n\nArab media reported that on 30 November 2016, Israeli jets allegedly struck a Syrian military compound in Damascus and a Hezbollah weapons convoy on the Damascus-Beirut highway.[[47]](#cite_note-50)\n\nOn 7 December 2016, Syria and Hezbollah accused Israel of launching [surface-to-surface missiles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surface-to-surface_missile \"Surface-to-surface missile\") targeting the [Mezzeh airbase](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mezzeh_Military_Airport \"Mezzeh Military Airport\") near Damascus. Unnamed Syrian sources told the Lebanese newspaper Elnashra that the strikes targeted the airport's [runway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runway \"Runway\") and operations command center, while another unnamed source said that the strikes targeted the regime's [4th division](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4th_Armoured_Division_(Syria) \"4th Armoured Division (Syria)\") op\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
