y | Ali Khamenei |\n| Chairman | * [Mahmoud Hashemi Shahroudi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahmoud_Hashemi_Shahroudi \"Mahmoud Hashemi Shahroudi\") * Sadeq Larijani |\n| [Prosecutor-General of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecutor-General_of_Iran \"Prosecutor-General of Iran\") | |\n| **In office** 23 August 2014 – 1 April 2016 | |\n| Appointed by | Sadeq Larijani |\n| Preceded by | [Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholam-Hossein_Mohseni-Eje'i \"Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Mohammad Jafar Montazeri](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Jafar_Montazeri \"Mohammad Jafar Montazeri\") |\n| [Member of the Assembly of Experts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_of_Experts \"Assembly of Experts\") | |\n| **In office** 24 May 2016 – 19 May 2024 | |\n| Constituency | [South Khorasan Province](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Khorasan_Province \"South Khorasan Province\") |\n| Majority | 325,139 (80%)[[1]](#cite_note-alef-2) |\n| **In office** 20 February 2007 – 21 May 2016 | |\n| Constituency | South Khorasan Province |\n| Majority | 200,906 (69%) |\n| 2nd [First Deputy Chief Justice of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Vice_Chief_Justice_of_Iran \"First Vice Chief Justice of Iran\") | |\n| **In office** 27 July 2004 – 23 August 2014 | |\n| [Chief Justice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Justice_of_Iran \"Chief Justice of Iran\") | Mahmoud Hashemi ShahroudiSadeq Larijani |\n| Preceded by | Mohammad-Hadi Marvi[[2]](#cite_note-3) |\n| Succeeded by | Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i |\n| Chairman of [General Inspection Office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Inspection_Office_(Iran) \"General Inspection Office (Iran)\") | |\n| **In office** 22 August 1994 – 9 August 2004 | |\n| Appointed by | [Mohammad Yazdi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Yazdi \"Mohammad Yazdi\") |\n| Preceded by | [Mostafa Mohaghegh Damad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mostafa_Mohaghegh_Damad \"Mostafa Mohaghegh Damad\") |\n| Succeeded by | Mohammad Niazi |\n| Personal details | |\n| Born | Ebrahim Raisolsadati 14 December 1960  [Mashhad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad \"Mashhad\"), Khorasan, Iran |\n| Died | 19 May 2024 (aged 63)  Near [Uzi, East Azerbaijan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uzi,_Varzaqan \"Uzi, Varzaqan\"), Iran |\n| Cause of death | [Helicopter crash](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Varzaqan_helicopter_crash \"2024 Varzaqan helicopter crash\") |\n| Resting place | [Imam Reza Shrine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam_Reza_Shrine \"Imam Reza Shrine\") |\n| [Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_party \"Political party\") | [Combatant Clergy Association](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combatant_Clergy_Association \"Combatant Clergy Association\")[[3]](#cite_note-bio-4) |\n| Other political affiliations | [Islamic Republican Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republican_Party \"Islamic Republican Party\") (until 1987)[[3]](#cite_note-bio-4) |\n| Spouse | [Jamileh Alamolhoda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamileh_Alamolhoda \"Jamileh Alamolhoda\")  ​  ​  (m. 1983)​ |\n| Children | 2 |\n| Relatives | [Ahmad Alamolhoda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad_Alamolhoda \"Ahmad Alamolhoda\") (father-in-law) |\n| [Alma mater](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alma_mater \"Alma mater\") | [Shahid Motahari University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahid_Motahari_University \"Shahid Motahari University\")[[3]](#cite_note-bio-4)[Qom Seminary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qom_Seminary \"Qom Seminary\")[[3]](#cite_note-bio-4)(both disputed)[[4]](#cite_note-BBC_News_فارسی-2021-5) |\n| Signature |  |\n| Website | [raisi.ir](https://raisi.ir/) |\n| Nickname | *Butcher of Tehran*[[5]](#cite_note-6)[[6]](#cite_note-7)[[7]](#cite_note-8) |\n|  | |\n\n\n\n**Ebrahim Raisolsadati**[[b]](#cite_note-9) (14 December 1960 – 19 May 2024), better known as **Ebrahim Raisi**,[[c]](#cite_note-10) was an Iranian [cleric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleric \"Cleric\") and politician who served as the eighth [president of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Iran \"President of Iran\") from 2021 until [his death in a helicopter crash](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Varzaqan_helicopter_crash \"2024 Varzaqan helicopter crash\") in 2024. A protégé of supreme leader [Ali Khamenei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\") and a [Principlist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_principlists \"Iranian principlists\"), Raisi was the second and most recent Iranian president to die in office after [Mohammad-Ali Rajai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad-Ali_Rajai \"Mohammad-Ali Rajai\").\n\nRaisi was the son-in-law of [Mashhad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad \"Mashhad\") [Friday prayer leader](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam_of_Friday_Prayer \"Imam of Friday Prayer\") and Grand Imam of [Imam Reza shrine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam_Reza_shrine \"Imam Reza shrine\"), [Ahmad Alamolhoda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad_Alamolhoda \"Ahmad Alamolhoda\"). He began his clerical studies at age 15. In the aftermath of the 1979 [Iranian Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\"), Raisi served in several positions in [Iran's judicial system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judicial_system_of_Iran \"Judicial system of Iran\"), including as Prosecutor of [Karaj](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karaj \"Karaj\"), Prosecutor of [Hamadan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamadan \"Hamadan\") and Deputy Prosecutor and Prosecutor of [Tehran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran \"Tehran\"). Raisi was part of the Tehran branch of what has been called the \"[1988 Iran death commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1988_executions_of_Iranian_political_prisoners \"1988 executions of Iranian political prisoners\")\". Under the direction of [Grand Ayatollah Khomeini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruhollah_Khomeini \"Ruhollah Khomeini\"), thousands of Iranian political prisoners were executed by these commissions,[[8]](#cite_note-11)[[9]](#cite_note-12): 15 and as a result of his involvement, Raisi earned himself the nickname: \"Butcher of Tehran\"[[10]](#cite_note-Da_Silva-2024-13) and was accused by [United Nations special rapporteurs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_special_rapporteur \"United Nations special rapporteur\") and other organizations of [crimes against humanity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity \"Crimes against humanity\"). He was Deputy Chief Justice (2004–2014), [Attorney General](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attorney-General_of_Iran \"Attorney-General of Iran\") (2014–2016), and [Chief Justice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Justice_of_Iran \"Chief Justice of Iran\") (2019–2021). Raisi was elected to the [Assembly of Experts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_of_Experts \"Assembly of Experts\") from [South Khorasan Province](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Khorasan_Province \"South Khorasan Province\"), for the first time in the [2006 election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_Iranian_Assembly_of_Experts_election \"2006 Iranian Assembly of Experts election\"). He was Custodian and Chairman of [Astan Quds Razavi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astan_Quds_Razavi \"Astan Quds Razavi\"), a [bonyad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonyad \"Bonyad\"), from 2016 until 2019.\n\nRaisi ran for [president in 2017](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Iranian_presidential_election \"2017 Iranian presidential election\") as the candidate of the conservative [Popular Front of Islamic Revolution Forces](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popular_Front_of_Islamic_Revolution_Forces \"Popular Front of Islamic Revolution Forces\"), losing to the moderate incumbent president [Hassan Rouhani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassan_Rouhani \"Hassan Rouhani\"), 57% to 38%. Raisi successfully ran for president a second time in 2021 with 63% of the votes, succeeding Rouhani. Considered a hardliner in Iranian politics, Raisi's presidency saw deadlock in negotiations with the U.S. over the [Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Comprehensive_Plan_of_Action \"Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action\") (JCPOA) and large-scale [protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests \"Mahsa Amini protests\") throughout the country in late 2022, triggered by the [death of Mahsa Amini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Mahsa_Amini \"Death of Mahsa Amin\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
