{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Saudi_Arabia_relations"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Saudi_Arabia_relations:\nIran–Saudi Arabia relations\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Map indicating locations of Iran and Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Iran_Saudi_Arabia_Locator.svg \"Map indicating locations of Iran and Saudi Arabia\") | |\n|  | |\n|  |  |\n| [Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran \"Iran\") | [Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia \"Saudi Arabia\") |\n| Diplomatic mission | |\n| Iranian Embassy, Riyadh | [Saudi Arabian Embassy, Tehran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_Saudi_Arabia,_Tehran \"Embassy of Saudi Arabia, Tehran\") |\n| Envoy | |\n| [Ambassador](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_Iran_to_Saudi_Arabia \"List of ambassadors of Iran to Saudi Arabia\") Alireza Enayati | [Ambassador](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_Saudi_Arabia_to_Iran \"List of ambassadors of Saudi Arabia to Iran\") Abdullah bin Saud al-Anzi |\n\nBilateral relations between [Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran \"Iran\") and [Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia \"Saudi Arabia\") have experienced periods of tension as well as phases of diplomatic engagement and normalization. The tensions have been attributed to a range of geopolitical issues, including regional influence, oil production policies, and differing relationships with the United States and other Western countries. [Diplomatic relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diplomatic_relations \"Diplomatic relations\") were suspended from 1987 to 1990, and they were more recently suspended from 2016 to 2023 again following certain issues like the intervention in [Yemen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yemen \"Yemen\"), Iran embassy bombing in Yemen, [incidents during the 2015 Hajj](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Mina_stampede#Increased_Saudi%E2%80%93Iranian_tensions \"2015 Mina stampede\"), the [execution of Nimr al-Nimr](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Nimr_al-Nimr \"Execution of Nimr al-Nimr\"), and the [attack on the Saudi diplomatic missions in Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_attack_on_the_Saudi_diplomatic_missions_in_Iran \"2016 attack on the Saudi diplomatic missions in Iran\"). However, in March 2023, after [discussions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Iran%E2%80%93Saudi_Arabia_summit \"2023 Iran–Saudi Arabia summit\") brokered by [China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\"), Iran and Saudi Arabia agreed to reestablish relations. In October 2024, Saudi Arabia and Iran made efforts to improve their relations. In a meeting in Doha, Qatar, Iranian President [Masoud Pezeshkian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian \"Masoud Pezeshkian\") and Saudi Arabia’s Foreign Minister [Faisal bin Farhan Al Saud](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faisal_bin_Farhan_Al_Saud \"Faisal bin Farhan Al Saud\") discussed the promotion of bilateral ties, [Israeli](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israel \"Israel\") attacks on [Gaza](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_Strip \"Gaza Strip\") and [Lebanon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon \"Lebanon\"), and Iran’s retaliatory attacks on Israel. The two sides emphasized the need to set aside their differences and work towards the expansion of relations.[[1]](#cite_note-2023resume-1)[[2]](#cite_note-nytimes_Saudi_Arabia_and_Iran_Agree_to_Restore_Ties-2)[[3]](#cite_note-3)\n\nSaudi Arabia is an absolute monarchy established in 1932 and has maintained close strategic relations with the United States and the United Kingdom since the post-World War II period.. Iran's government alternated between absolute monarchy and increasing parliamentarianism, until the [1953 Iranian coup d'état](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1953_Iranian_coup_d'%C3%A9tat \"1953 Iranian coup d'état\") increased [Mohammad Reza Pahlavi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Reza_Pahlavi \"Mohammad Reza Pahlavi\")'s powers as monarch within a parliamentary system. The coup was supported by the army, the bazaar, the clerics[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*], the United States and the United Kingdom. Both states were aligned with the [Western Bloc](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Bloc \"Western Bloc\") in the [Cold War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War \"Cold War\") against the [Soviet Union](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_Union \"Soviet Union\"), until the end of formal [Iran-United States relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran-United_States_relations \"Iran-United States relations\") in 1980 following the [Iranian Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\"). Throughout both the Cold War and the [war on terror](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_on_terror \"War on terror\"), the United States has continued to pursue a [strategic relationship with Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia-United_States_relations \"Saudi Arabia-United States relations\").\n\nBoth countries are major oil and gas exporters and have clashed over [energy policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_policy \"Energy policy\"). Saudi Arabia, with its large oil reserves and smaller population, has a greater interest in taking a long-term view of the global oil market and incentive to moderate prices. In contrast, Iran is compelled to focus on high prices in the short term due to its low standard of living given recent sanctions after its [decade-old war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Iraq_War \"Iran–Iraq War\") with [Saddam Hussein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saddam_Hussein \"Saddam Hussein\")'s [Iraq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ba'athist_Iraq \"Ba'athist Iraq\") and its larger population.[[4]](#cite_note-Wehrey-4)\n\nIn the [Syrian civil war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_civil_war \"Syrian civil war\"), [Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_involvement_in_the_Syrian_Civil_War \"Iranian involvement in the Syrian Civil War\") has supported the Syrian government militarily and with billions of dollars of aid, while Saudi Arabia was a [major supplier of aid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabian_support_to_Syrian_Opposition_in_the_Syrian_Civil_War \"Saudi Arabian support to Syrian Opposition in the Syrian Civil War\") to rebel groups. Both governments have accused each other of supporting militant or extremist groups.[[5]](#cite_note-5)[[6]](#cite_note-6) Iran and Saudi Arabia have competing interests in [the battle for dominance over their region](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Saudi_Arabia_proxy_conflict \"Iran–Saudi Arabia proxy conflict\").[[7]](#cite_note-7)\n\nAs of 2026, the relationship between the two countries became once again strained and hostile following [2026 Iranian strikes on Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iranian_strikes_on_Saudi_Arabia \"2026 Iranian strikes on Saudi Arabia\") as part of [2026 Iran war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war \"2026 Iran war\") which also targeted oil refineries and civil infrastructures inside Saudi Arabia.\n\nAfter the [Iranian Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\"), relations deteriorated considerably after Iran accused Saudi Arabia of aligning itself with the United States in the [Persian Gulf](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_Gulf \"Persian Gulf\") region, representing U.S. interests rather than what it perceived to be those of the [Muslim world](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muslim_world \"Muslim world\"). Saudi Arabia is concerned by Iran's consistent desire to export its revolution across the Persian Gulf region—notably in post-Saddam [Iraq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iraq \"Iraq\"), the Levant, and further south. It is also opposed to Iran's controversial [nuclear program](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_nuclear_program \"Iranian nuclear program\").[[4]](#cite_note-Wehrey-4)\n\nTensions between the two countries have waxed and waned. Relations between Saudi Arabia and Iran soured particularly after the [Iranian Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution \"Iranian Revolution\"), the commencement of the Iranian [nuclear program](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_nuclear_program \"Iranian nuclear program\"), the [2011 alleged Iran assassination plot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_alleged_Iran_assassination_plot \"2011 alleged Iran assassination plot\") and the 2016 execution of [Nimr al-Nimr](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nimr_al-Nimr \"Nimr al-Nimr\"). There have also been numerous attempts to improve the relationship. After the 1991 Gulf War there was a noticeable thaw in relations.[[8]](#cite_note-bradley-expo--8) In March 2007, [President Ahmadinejad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_Ahmadinejad \"President Ahmadinejad\") of Iran visited [Riyadh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riyadh \"Riyadh\") and was greeted at the airport by [King Abdullah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdullah_bin_Abdulaziz_Al_Saud_(1931%E2%80%932015) \"Abdullah bin Abdulaziz Al Saud (1931–2015)\"), and the two countries were referred to in the press as \"brotherly nations\".[[9]](#cite_note-9)\n\nSince March 2011, the [Syrian Civil War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_civil_war \"Syrian civil war\") has also been a source of tension. Iran has provided financial and military support for the [Syrian government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_government \"Syrian government\"), led by [Bashar al-Assad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bashar_al-Assad \"Bashar al-Assad\"), while Saudi Arabia has supported [Syrian rebels](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_armed_factions_in_the_Syrian_Civil_War \"List of armed factions in the Syrian Civil War\"), as well as [Al Qaeda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al_Qaeda \"Al Qaeda\") and [ISIS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISIS \"ISIS\") even. [[10]](#cite_note-10)\n\nOn 3 January 2016, Saudi Arabia's embassy in Tehran, Iran was ransacked following the [execution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Nimr_al-Nimr \"Execution of Nimr al-Nimr\") of Saudi-born [Shia Islam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shia_Islam \"Shia Islam\") cle\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
