sband.\" Civil code allows girls to marry at 13.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 6B.3, 6B.8 (dignity/equal opportunity)\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n### S005 — Honoring killings / lack of domestic-violence law (HRW)\n- provisional_id: `S005-Honor-killings-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:hrw-world-report-2024-iran + claim:165-women-killed-2021-2023`\n- locator: HRW 2024 citing Shargh newspaper and official statistics\n- claim: At least 165 women in Iran were killed by male family members between March 2021 and end of June 2023 (Shargh, official statistics). From mid-March to mid-May 2023 alone, 27 women were murdered in so-called \"honor killings.\" Iran has no law on domestic violence to prevent abuse and protect survivors.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 6B.3, 6B.7, 6B.8\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n### S006 — Mohammadi/Nobel-laureate imprisonment (2023 Nobel Peace Prize to Narges Mohammadi)\n- provisional_id: `S006-Narges-Mohammadi-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:freedomhouse.org/country/iran/freedom-world-2023 + claim:mohammadi-imprisoned-2023`\n- locator: Freedom House 2023\n- claim: Narges Mohammadi, prominent human rights defender, remained behind bars; she was awarded the 2023 Nobel Peace Prize.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 6B.1, 6B.3, 6B.7\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n**Chapter 6B lens coverage synthesis:** Coverage is patchy because rulers in Iran generally do not have authority over basic welfare delivery — most basic welfare targets (education, health) are inherited institutional baselines, not 2023 ruler-attributed choices. The strongest ruler-attributed welfare events are the systematic harassment of women's rights defenders (S003), preservation of statutory discrimination (S004), tolerance of \"honor killings\" without legislative reform (S005), and absence of protective action during the schoolgirl poisoning wave (D003 / S001).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Chapter 7B — Personal Integrity & Honesty\n\n**Status of local priors:** LF012 (accountability −0.735), LF014 (BTI governance 1.56), LF015 (BTI status 2.57), LF028 (rule of law 0.185), LF033 (corruption index 0.787), LF034 (CPI 24/100), LF035 (exec corruption 0.723), LF036 (public corruption 0.659). Country-level context only — ruler-specific integrity claims need narrative attribution.\n\n**Items added to ledger:**\n\n### Item Y001 — Khamenei family's documented presence in ruling-network economic structure\n- provisional_id: `Y001-Khamenei-family-economic-role`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:multiple-secondary-analyses + claim:family-network-business-presence`\n- locator: Various analytic sources (post-2009, but consistently referenced in 2023 analyses)\n- claim: Khamenei's relatives (especially his daughter and son Mojtaba Khamenei) have been the subject of multiple investigative reports alleging they control large economic networks in Iran, including real estate, oil, and finance sectors. Mojtaba Khamenei was sanctioned by the US Treasury in November 2020 (pre-period) under EO 13876 for personal enrichment and corruption; he remained in place through 2023.\n- source_type / confidence: investigative reporting (Reuters, AP); Treasury press release; `medium_high`\n- ruler attribution: direct family; reported by reputable outlets\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.3, 7B.4, 7B.5, 7B.9\n- disposition: `final_evidence` (the sanction facts); `context` for the analytic claims (which are allegations repeatedly documented but require care)\n\n### Item Y002 — Transparency International CPI 2023: Iran score 24/100\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.5, 7B.7, 7B.9\n- Reuse: LF034 — `final_evidence`\n\n### Item Y003 — V-Dem corruption indicators, 2023\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.5, 7B.9 — `final_evidence`\n\n### Y004 — Investigative findings of Iranian political-corruption networks\n- provisional_id: `Y004-Iran-oligarchic-networks-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:various-IRGC-affiliated-contracting-reports + claim:irgc-affiliated-economic-role-2023`\n- locator: Multiple post-2020 sources including FATF grey-listing and Iran-watcher analyses\n- claim: Throughout 2023, IRGC-affiliated entities (Khatam al-Anbiya, Bonyad Mostazafen, etc.) retained major roles in Iran's contracting economy, oil/construction/finance sectors, despite FATF grey-listing and partial sanctions pressure. Khamenei (as Supreme Leader and IRGC commander-in-chief) has authority over appointments.\n- source_type / confidence: think-tank/independent analysis; `medium`\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.5, 7B.9\n- disposition: `context`\n\n### Y005 — Narges Mohammadi 2023 Nobel and the regime's refusal to release\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.7 (state power to conceal and punish investigators)\n- Reuse with reference to freedomhouse 2023\n\n### Y006 — Schoolgirl poisoning cover-up investigation failure\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 7B.7, 7B.6 (independent investigation tolerance)\n- Reuse D003\n\n**Chapter 7B lens coverage synthesis:** Lens coverage is structurally adequate but most claims involve alleged corruption rather than direct documented ruler-perpetrated acts. The Mojtaba Khamenei US Treasury sanctions are the most cleanly ruler-attributed integrity fact carried forward into 2023. The CPI 24/100 country score is context.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Chapter 8B — Ruler Effectiveness & Competence\n\n**Status of local priors:** LF012 (accountability), LF014 (BTI governance), LF028 (rule of law). Country-context priors only.\n\n**Items added to ledger:**\n\n### Item X001 — Khamenei's stated program: continuation of resistance-economy + control levers\n- provisional_id: `X001-Khamenei-program-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:khamenei.ir-2023-speeches + claim:continuity-doctrine`\n- locator: khamenei.ir annual messages\n- claim: Khamenei repeatedly defined his 2023 program as: (a) preservation of the Islamic Republic; (b) resistance-economy drive to reduce external vulnerability; (c) leveraging nuclear and regional posture for sanctions relief; (d) defending the \"resistance axis.\" He reiterated these themes across multiple major speeches (e.g., Nowruz address, lecturing meetings with state officials).\n- source_type / confidence: official primary; `medium_high`\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.6\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n### X002 — Khamenei direct management of nuclear posture (N001–N005)\n- Cross-reference from 1B; demonstrates actor-level decision to escalate (e.g., 84% finding) and reinforce red lines (N003, N004).\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.3, 8B.4, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.10\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n### X003 — Iran-Saudi détente (I002) — implementation through Khamenei-period envoy Shamkhani\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.3, 8B.4, 8B.7, 8B.8\n- Reuse I002 as evidence Khamenei-period foreign policy executed a defined initiative end-to-end.\n\n### X004 — March 2023 amnesty and judicial handling (HRW 2024 / IRNA)\n- provisional_id: `X004-Mass-amnesty-March-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:irna.ir/news/85055898 + claim:Eslam-Ejeyi-said-22000-amnestied-13-Mar-2023`\n- locator: IRNA via HRW\n- claim: On 13 Mar 2023, head of judiciary Eslam Ejeyi stated that 22,000 people were included in the amnesty orders related to protests. The amnesty excluded human-rights defenders with lengthy sentences and those facing capital charges.\n- target-period fit: 2023\n- ruler attribution: judiciary head is appointed by the Supreme Leader.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.10\n- disposition: `final_evidence`\n\n### X005 — Raising of public trust / legitimacy by Hormuz and Strait of Hormuz leverage signalling\n- provisional_id: `X005-Strait-of-Hormuz-leverage-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:secondary-iran-2023-leverage-signalling + claim:Iran-strait-leverage-2023`\n- locator: Various think-tank retrospectives; minimal 2023-dated specific Khamenei statement\n- claim: Throughout 2023 Iranian leadership publicly warned that it would close the Strait of Hormuz or use other leverage against Western pressure. Specific Khamenei-authored statements exist but are not led by a unique 2023 issue that is materially distinct from a long-running policy.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.7, 8B.10\n- disposition: `context`\n\n### X006 — Failure of iterative adaptation (rejected candidates):\n- Renewal of protests in 2023 was met by intensification of repression rather than accommodation; economic policy produced no recovery.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.8 (counter-adaptation evidence)\n- disposition: `final_evidence` (negative adaptation evidence)\n\n### X007 — Khamenei succession resilience — Assembly of Experts\n- provisional_id: `X007-Assembly-of-Experts-2023`\n- canonical_fact_key: `loc:en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_of_Experts + claim:composition-and-selection-rigidity`\n- locator: Wikipedia: Assembly of Experts (general)\n- claim: The Assembly of Experts (which selects and could replace the Supreme Leader) remains entirely composed of clerics vetted through the Guardian Council. Reformist gains in 2024 Assembly elections were modest (post-period context only). In 2023 the body was not in election cycle but its composition reflects Khamenei-aligned vetting.\n- candidate chapters/lenses: 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.9\n- disposition: `context`\n\n**Chapter 8B lens coverage synthesis:**\n- 8B.1 program identification: explicit (`resistance economy`, control levers) — `final_evidence`\n- 8B.2 operationalization: partial — concrete actions (amnesty, JCPOA-frozen posture, IRGC appointments, hijab bill approval, drone transfers)\n- 8B.3 mobilization: weakly evidenced — IRGC, Basij, judiciary coordinated around protest suppression efficiently\n- 8B.4 personnel selection: weakly evidenced — judiciary head is Khamenei appointment; loyalist commanders in place\n- 8B.5 coordination: weakly evidenced — repression apparatus coordinated; economic policy fragmented\n- 8B.6 observable implementation: mixed (some projects (3B amnesty executed), but with significant exclusion list)\n- 8B.7 outcome indicators moved favorably: no — inflation/rile/HUMAN-rights continued to decline; nuclear stockpile grew\n- 8B.8 adaptation: weak — protests brought intensification not accommodation\n- 8B.9 crisis management: very mixed — Iran-Saudi détente a positive, Gaza war preceded without committing to direct war\n- 8B.10 end-state trajectory: mixed — regional diplomatic gain (Saudi) but domestic control costs high, no economic recovery, posture more dangerous end-of-period on nuclear\n- ruler_attribution check: most policies were indeed directed by Khamenei; specific Raisi-led ones belong to Raisi but the strategic apex is Khamenei.\n- morality_separation_check: program execution is being evaluated, including harmful programs, without moral re-judgment per rubric.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-chapter reuse map (no double-counting)\n\n| Evidence item | Reused across chapters |\n|---|---|\n| D003 (schoolgirl poisoning) | 3B + 6B + 7B |\n| P006 (Baha'i persecution) | 4B + 7B (latter as welfare harm) |\n| S003 (women's rights defenders) | 3B + 6B |\n| D005 / P007 (Hijab & Chastity Bill) | 3B + 4B |\n| Y001 (Mojtaba Khamenei US sanctions) | 7B primary |\n| N001/N002/N003/N004/N005 (nuclear) | 1B primary; N002 also 7B |\n| I002 (Iran-Saudi détente) | 2B + 8B |\n| I003 (drone transfers to Russia) | 2B + (8B operationalization) |\n| D002 (executions) | 3B + (7B institutional opacity) |\n| LF012 accountability + LF028 rule of law | 4B + 7B + 8B (priors) |\n| LF015 BTI status | 4B + 5B (priors) |\n\n---\n\n## Source mix and family diversity, by chapter\n\n- **Chapter 1B:** Intergovernmental IAEA reports via UN-SCR/Washington-institute analysis; regime self-presentation via khamenei.ir + Iran Primer; media wires (Reuters/AP/France 24). Families ≥ 3.\n- **Chapter 2B:** Wikipedia encyclopedic synthesis (corroborative); MFA-China primary (Iran-Saudi); HRW; Valdai + think-tanks; Media (WSJ/LongWar). Families ≥ 3.\n- **Chapter 3B:** HRW (ngo, medium_high); IHR/HRANA (ngo); Freedom House (