que combination of source type + time + claim, even if referenced again in a different chapter context (cross-chapter reuse is allowed per the rubric; only same-URL duplication within a chapter is flagged, and none appears here).\n\n---\n\n## Strongest contrary evidence collected\n\n- Iran called the 84% uranium finding a \"conspiracy\" / \"unintended technical fluctuation\" — contrary to IAEA technical readings (N001).\n- Iranian government position that the hijab law's broader enforcement protects social order, contrary to human-rights organizations' documentation that it caused Garavand's death and broader intimidation (D004, D005).\n- Iranian government's explanation of schoolgirl poisonings as inconclusive/investigating, contrary to pattern described by HRW (D003).\n- Iran's position that the fatwa forbids nuclear weapons, contrary to analytic skepticism (N002).\n- Government characterization of the protests as \"riots\"/\"hybrid war\" by foreign enemies, contrary to social-research characterizations of rooted domestic grievance (Iran Primer, HRW).\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved gaps and search blocks\n\n- **1B.6 proliferation by allies, 1B.7 experts vs. loyalists, 1B.9 dual-use tech:** Could not surface 2023-specific authoritative evidence in the time/page-budget available; further research would help.\n- **6B pandemic/disaster response:** No major 2023 pandemic event; COVID-era response was largely pre-period; lens 6B.6 should be treated as no-2023-discriminator.\n- **7B.6 tolerance of independent investigation:** Evidence overwhelmingly negative (Mohammadi, schoolgirl poisoning, Raisi's 1988 past). Documented evidence exists but the picture is starkly one-sided, which should warn against treating silence as integrity.\n- **8B.4 personnel capability and 8B.7 specific outcome indicators by program branch:** Would benefit from additional IMF, World Bank, and central-bank-of-Iran primary documents for execution depth.\n\n---\n\n## Local-data audit (retained vs. unused)